WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.423
A shopper pays once, but gets three order confirmation emails.

00:00:04.423 --> 00:00:08.847
Why? The payment provider delivered the same webhook three times.

00:00:08.847 --> 00:00:15.482
A timeout or lost response can trigger retries even after your server saved the payment.

00:00:15.482 --> 00:00:19.464
Webhooks are usually at least once, not exactly once.

00:00:19.464 --> 00:00:21.675
Here is the safe design.

00:00:21.675 --> 00:00:25.657
First, verify the signature using the raw request body.

00:00:25.657 --> 00:00:31.850
Next, insert the provider event ID into a PostgreSQL inbox with a unique constraint.

00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:40.255
In that same transaction, update the payment only if its state allows the transition, and write an outbox event.

00:00:40.255 --> 00:00:45.563
If the event ID already exists, acknowledge it without repeating the work.

00:00:45.563 --> 00:00:52.641
After commit, an asynchronous worker sends the email or fulfillment command using its own idempotency key.

00:00:52.641 --> 00:00:56.622
If that worker crashes, the outbox lets it retry.

00:00:56.622 --> 00:01:01.046
Watch duplicate delivery rate, outbox lag, and payment state mismatches.

00:01:01.046 --> 00:01:08.566
The lesson: one webhook can arrive many times, but the business action must be safe to repeat.
